{"seq": 56, "at": "2026-10-31T15:11:42Z", "issue": 94, "action": "update_view", "actor": "prod.mf", "owner": "mf", "entity": "ideas/ccc-gtcr-take-private-2026/views/prod.mf", "version": 1, "target_date": "2027-03-31", "proposal_sha256": "0e06a12e77e8de76ac2d2b24fe3ca07fe147daa882df9c81c21a4dca335f23a8", "diff": {"position": [null, "neutral"], "strategy": [null, "announced-transaction"], "sub_strategy": [null, "cash-merger"], "horizon_months": [null, 5], "target_date": [null, "2027-03-31"], "confidence": [null, 0.35], "methodology": [null, "koumei"], "non_public_pillars": [null, []], "derived.expected_price": [null, 7.596], "derived.expected_return": [null, -0.007059], "derived.p10": [null, 7.2], "derived.p50": [null, 7.75], "derived.p90": [null, 7.75]}, "rationale": "Initial view: standing aside. The transaction has the shape this method looks for, a fixed cash payoff and a dated outcome, and the consideration is established in a binding agreement. What is missing is the evidence that would make the spread interpretable: no definitive proxy statement has been filed, so the outside date, the termination fees, the named regulatory jurisdictions and the stockholder meeting date are unknown, and the spread of about 1.3% leaves almost no compensation for a failure whose downside I cannot yet bound. Publishing a long view here would be a bet that an undisclosed process finishes on schedule, which is not a conclusion the documents support. I will reassess when the definitive proxy statement is filed: at that point k1, k2 and k6 can be assessed against named terms, and if the disclosure shows a wider spread than the terms justify, this view moves to long."}
{"seq": 57, "at": "2026-10-31T15:16:48Z", "issue": 96, "action": "add_evidence", "actor": "prod.mf", "owner": "mf", "entity": "evidence/ev-20261031-ccc-2026q3-standalone-financials"}
{"seq": 58, "at": "2026-10-31T15:22:23Z", "issue": 97, "action": "update_view", "actor": "prod.mf", "owner": "mf", "entity": "ideas/ccc-gtcr-take-private-2026/views/prod.mf", "version": 2, "target_date": "2027-03-31", "proposal_sha256": "a89eaec2af101c462ff1f6e07615351117d1171eb684d1b27ac572d7556ffb1a", "diff": {"confidence": [0.35, 0.4], "derived.expected_price": [7.596, 7.587], "derived.expected_return": [-0.007059, -0.008235], "derived.p10": [7.2, 6.37]}, "rationale": "Second version. The change is that the failure case is now measured rather than asserted. In the first version the $6.10 failure point was a marking convention with no derivation. Reading the quarterly report for the period ended 30 September 2026 supplies the missing input: annualised free cash flow of about $275 million before stock-based compensation, stock-based compensation of $93.8 million or 36% of operating cash flow, net debt of $1,078 million and 591.0 million shares. Valued at 12 to 16 times adjusted EBITDA of $413 million to $482 million, the standalone equity is worth about $6.57 to $11.23 a share, so the $7.75 consideration sits in roughly the bottom quarter of that range rather than 44% above it. That cuts both ways and both directions are recorded: termination costs less than the first version implied, so the failure point moves up from $6.10 to $6.37, and the thin spread is less attractive than it looked because there is less distressed upside on a break. The position stays neutral because the three unmet tests have not changed: the decisive terms are still unfiled, there is still no evidence gap to trade, and the time compensation is still negative against cash. No assumptions were widened to change the distribution shape; the four points are the output of the valuation above, and the failure point moves by the amount the standalone range implies."}
